"""SEN0401 chapter 5, corpus text part B (version 1.0.0): the branch 'Recovery codes' - the five schemes the chapter names and the new proposal
beside them, and the choices a reader has to make about a code (passphrase, deniability, memory, coercion, a wallet birthday).
Markers such as [AH] become author-year strings (sen0401_ch05_common)."""
from sen0401_ch05_common_v1_0_0 import *

MN12 = "army van defense carry jealous true garbage claim echo media make crunch"

NODES_B = [
 ("RecoveryCodes", "Recovery codes", 1, None, None, P(
  (W, "A recovery code is the short, written form of a wallet's seed: a sequence of words, or in one case a string of letters and digits, from which every key of a deterministic wallet can be computed again. The chapter introduces the term in its section on seeds and recovery codes, where it says that recovery codes are intended to make backups easier and safer, and it notes that although seeds are large random numbers, usually 128 to 256 bits, most recovery codes use human-language words [AH]. This branch is about the codes themselves: the schemes in wide use, what each adds, and the decisions a user makes when they write one down."),
  (Y, "The branch matters because the recovery code is the one thing a user is asked to keep, and keeping it is the whole of the backup. The chapter states both sides of that plainly: if a wallet database is corrupted or lost, every private key can be regenerated from the original seed, but if someone else obtains the seed they can generate the same private keys and steal all the bitcoins from a single-signature wallet [AH]. A code is therefore a single object that has to survive fire, flood and forgetfulness while resisting theft and coercion, and those requirements pull against each other."),
  (E, "Recovery codes are met the first time a modern wallet application is started. The chapter says that a wallet implementing deterministic wallets with a BIP39 recovery code shows the user a sequence of 12 to 24 words when the wallet is first created, and that this sequence of words is the wallet backup [AH]. Other applications show other kinds: the Electrum wallet from version 2.0 on, the lnd wallet with its 24-word cipher seed, the Muun wallet with a code that accompanies a printed kit, and hardware signing devices that offer the shares of a split code [EL] [AZ] [MU] [S39]."),
  (H, "The branch is organised in two groups. The first group, the recovery code schemes, takes the five kinds the chapter lists as in wide use, BIP39, Electrum version 2, Aezeed, Muun and SLIP39, and adds the proposal the chapter mentions in a note, codex32; each section says what the scheme encodes, what it adds over BIP39, and what it costs [AH] [B93]. The second group collects the choices that cut across the schemes: whether to use a passphrase, what plausible deniability buys and costs, why a chosen phrase is not a recovery code, what memory can and cannot be trusted with, what a code is worth to someone willing to use force, and what a wallet birthday saves during a restoration."),
  (K, "Two confusions should be cleared away before the detail begins. The first is that a recovery code is not a password: it is not chosen by the user, it is generated from randomness by the wallet, and it cannot be changed without moving the money, except in the one scheme that enciphers its own seed [AZ]. The second is that the schemes are not interchangeable. The chapter says that some solutions have almost no downsides and are universally adopted, while others involve trade-offs on which wallet authors differ, and it recommends only the first kind as best practice [AH]; which code a user holds therefore determines which software can read it.")), None),

 ("CodeSchemes", "Recovery code schemes", 2, "RecoveryCodes", None, P(
  (W, "This group describes the recovery code schemes themselves. The chapter lists five as being in wide use at the time of writing, BIP39, Electrum version 2, Aezeed, Muun and SLIP39, and adds a note on a sixth proposal, codex32, which appeared while the book was being written [AH]. Each section of the group explains what the scheme turns into words, what defect of BIP39 it was designed to repair, and what it asks of the software that has to read the code back."),
  (Y, "A student who learns only one scheme will misread the others. The schemes differ in whether the words encode the seed itself or a ciphertext, in whether the code carries a version number, in whether its checksum covers the passphrase, and in whether one code is enough or several must be collected. Those differences decide what happens in the one situation that matters, the restoration, and they explain why a code written by one wallet is sometimes refused by another."),
  (H, "The sections are ordered as the chapter orders them, beginning with the scheme that the rest are measured against. BIP39 is treated first and in most detail, because the second half of the chapter builds its whole technology stack on it; Electrum version 2 and Aezeed each remove one of its shortcomings; Muun shows a code that is not a seed at all; SLIP39 and codex32 split one secret into several codes. A learner who has read the group can say, for any code in their hand, what it encodes and what software will be needed to use it.")), None),

 ("Bip39Code", "BIP39 recovery code", 3, "CodeSchemes", ("12 words for 128 bits of entropy",
   "A BIP39 recovery code is a sequence of 12 to 24 words that encodes a random number together with a short checksum, from which a 512-bit seed is derived; the standard was published in 2013 and is the most widely used scheme.",
   ("(128 + 128 // 32) // 11", "12")), P(
  (W, "A BIP39 recovery code is a word sequence that represents, that is encodes, a random number used as a seed from which a deterministic wallet is derived [AH]. The standard that defines it, BIP39, describes the implementation of a mnemonic code or mnemonic sentence, a group of easy-to-remember words, for the generation of deterministic wallets, and says that it consists of two parts: generating the mnemonic and converting it into a binary seed [B39]. The chapter sums up the procedure in one line: the wallet generates a random sequence of bytes, adds a checksum to it, and encodes the data into a series of 12 to 24 words, which may be localised to the user's language [AH]."),
  (Y, "The scheme exists because people transcribe words better than hexadecimal. The standard's own motivation says that a mnemonic sentence is superior for human interaction compared with the handling of raw binary or hexadecimal representations of a wallet seed, and that such a sentence could be written on paper or spoken over the telephone [B39]. The chapter adds the practical consequence: recovery codes make it easier for users to back up because they are easy to read and correctly transcribe [AH]. The hexadecimal and word forms of the same code, printed side by side in the chapter, make the point better than any argument."),
  (E, "A reader meets BIP39 in nearly every wallet application and in several places in this course. The chapter chooses it, together with BIP32 derivation and BIP44-style paths, as the stack of technologies it thinks most widely used in wallets, and the second half of the chapter is about that stack [AH]. The lnd wallet documentation notes that its own cipher seed and a BIP39 phrase look similar and share only the English word list [LR]. Bitcoin Core, by contrast, does not implement BIP39 at all: its list of implemented proposals names BIP 32, 43, 44, 49, 84 and 86 but no BIP 39, and the wallet that release 31.1 created for this chapter has descriptors with extended keys and no recovery code [BC] [B31]."),
  (H, "The arithmetic of the code is fixed by two rules. The entropy must be a multiple of 32 bits and between 128 and 256 bits; the checksum is the first entropy-length over 32 bits of the SHA-256 hash of that entropy, appended to it; and the result is cut into groups of 11 bits, each group an index into a list of 2,048 words [B39]. For the smallest allowed size this gives the worked example: 128 bits of entropy plus 128 divided by 32, that is 4, checksum bits make 132 bits, and 132 divided by 11 is the 12 words that a wallet shows. The rest of the mechanism, the word list, the key-stretching function and the optional passphrase, has its own group later in this chapter."),
  (K, "BIP39 has shortcomings that the standard itself lists, and they are the reason the other schemes exist. The seed depends on the word list used, because the conversion hashes the sentence rather than the entropy, so translating a code to another language gives a different seed; the conversion is one-way, so an arbitrary BIP32 seed cannot be written as a BIP39 sentence; the checksum is short, so about one random error in 256 is missed and no correction is possible; and there is no versioning scheme, which the standard says is now largely mitigated by descriptor wallets [B39]. The chapter is blunter: BIP39 recovery codes have several shortcomings which later schemes attempt to address [AH].")), None),

 ("ElectrumV2Code", "Electrum version 2 recovery code", 3, "CodeSchemes", ("132 bits of entropy in 12 words",
   "The Electrum version 2 scheme derives both the master private key and a version number from a hash of the normalised phrase, so it needs no fixed word list and can tell an old code from a new one.",
   ("132 // 11", "12")), P(
  (W, "The Electrum version 2 recovery code is the word-based scheme used in the Electrum wallet from version 2.0 onwards. The chapter says that it has several advantages over BIP39: it does not rely on a global word list that every version of every compatible program must implement, and its codes include a version number that improves reliability and efficiency; like BIP39 it supports an optional passphrase, which Electrum calls a seed extension, and it uses the same key-stretching function [AH]. Electrum's own document is the source for the mechanism and calls the scheme its Seed Version System [EL]."),
  (Y, "Both advantages answer a real failure. A fixed word list means that every future version of every wallet has to ship the same list in order to read an old code, and Electrum's document says that this threatens the portability of BIP39 phrases, a problem made worse by one list per language [EL]. A missing version number means that software cannot tell which derivation a code belongs to; the document warns that a wallet which has dropped a deprecated derivation cannot detect that a phrase is unsupported and will return an empty wallet instead, which threatens users' funds [EL]."),
  (E, "A reader meets this scheme whenever an Electrum phrase has to be restored, and it is the reason Electrum refuses to generate BIP39 codes at all [EL]. The chapter's sidebar on how much entropy is needed records Electrum's figure among the others: BIP32 allows seeds of 128 to 512 bits, BIP39 accepts 128 to 256 bits of entropy, Electrum version 2 accepts 132 bits, Aezeed accepts 128, and SLIP39 either 128 or 256 [AH]. The worked example is that figure in words: 132 bits of entropy divided by 11 bits a word is the 12 words of a typical Electrum phrase, which the document confirms when it says that a typical seed has 12 words, giving 132 bits of entropy in the choice of the seed [EL]."),
  (H, "The version number is read out of a keyed hash of the phrase. The document gives the procedure: the phrase is normalised, reduced to single spaces between words with diacritics removed, then hashed with HMAC-SHA512 under the key 'Seed version'; the first hexadecimal digit of the result says how long the version prefix is, and the prefix itself is the version number [EL]. Three numbers are registered: 0x01 for standard pay-to-public-key-hash and multisignature wallets, 0x100 for segwit, and 0x101 for two-factor wallets [EL]. Generation works backwards from this rule: a nonce, a number varied for the purpose, is enumerated and the phrase re-hashed until the hash begins with the wanted prefix, so a phrase that produces no registered number is not a valid Electrum seed, which is how the version doubles as an integrity check [EL]."),
  (K, "The scheme's own document is careful about what the prefix costs. Imposing a prefix does not reduce the entropy of the seed, because an attacker gains no knowledge of the phrase, but it does let an attacker reject a wrong candidate before doing the key stretching, which reduces the strength that stretching adds; with Electrum's values the document works the cost out as 2 to the 132 plus 11 minus 8, that is about 2 to the 135 hashes [EL]. The scheme also shares BIP39's deeper limitation: its optional passphrase is not covered by any checksum, so a mistyped passphrase cannot be detected [AH].")), None),

 ("AezeedCode", "Aezeed cipher seed", 3, "CodeSchemes", ("19 plaintext bytes before enciphering",
   "The Aezeed scheme of the lnd wallet enciphers a plaintext of version, timestamp and entropy into a 24-word code, so the passphrase is authenticated and the code can be upgraded or re-enciphered.",
   ("1 + 2 + 16", "19")), P(
  (W, "Aezeed is the word-based recovery code of the lnd wallet, and the chapter presents it as another scheme offering improvements over BIP39. It says that Aezeed includes two version numbers, one internal, which removes several problems with upgrading wallet applications, and one external, which can be incremented to change the underlying cryptographic properties of the code; that it includes a wallet birthday, a reference to the date on which the wallet database was created; and that it supports changing the passphrase or other aspects of the code without moving funds to a new seed, the user needing only to back up a new code [AH]. The package's own document calls it the aezeed cipher seed scheme [AZ]."),
  (Y, "The scheme was written to fix exactly the two gaps the chapter names. Its document says that the new scheme aims to address two major features lacking in BIP39, versioning and a wallet birthday: without a version, wallets may not know how to re-derive addresses during recovery; without a birthday, they do not know how far back in the chain to look to derive all of the user's addresses [AZ]. It adds a third complaint, that BIP39 uses a very weak key derivation function, and uses scrypt with the parameters n equal to 32,768, r equal to 8 and p equal to 1 instead [AZ]. The chapter repeats the figure when it compares key-stretching parameters [AH]."),
  (E, "The scheme is met by anyone recovering an lnd node. The recovery document says that fund recovery requires two pieces of data, the 24-word cipher seed and the encrypted static channel backup file, and that only the first is needed if just the on-chain funds are wanted [LR]. It also warns that a BIP39 phrase and a cipher seed look similar but share only the default English word list of BIP39 [LR]. The worked example is the plaintext the scheme starts from, which its document gives as one byte of internal version, two bytes of timestamp and sixteen bytes of entropy: 1 plus 2 plus 16 is the 19 bytes that are then enciphered [AZ]."),
  (H, "The enciphering is what distinguishes the scheme. The document describes the steps: the external version is put at the front; scrypt with the version's parameters derives a 32-byte key from the user's passphrase with a five-byte salt, which it says makes the precomputation of keys infeasible, the purpose the salt section of this chapter describes; the 19-byte plaintext is enciphered with aez, an authenticated cipher whose checksum length is configurable and is set to 8 bytes, that is a 64-bit checksum, with the version and salt as associated data; and the resulting 33 bytes are encoded as 24 English words from BIP39's list [AZ]. If no passphrase is given, the string 'aezeed' is used instead [AZ]. Because the words are a ciphertext, the same seed can be re-enciphered under a new passphrase or new parameters, which is what upgrading a code means."),
  (K, "Authentication changes the trade-offs in a way the chapter discusses at length. Unlike the other schemes, Aezeed authenticates its optional passphrase and returns an error for a wrong value, which the chapter says eliminates plausible deniability, adds error detection and makes it possible to prove that the passphrase has been revealed [AH]. The scheme's document points the other way on the same fact: because the mnemonic is itself a ciphertext, leaving it in plaintext is advisable only if a passphrase is also set, in contrast with BIP39, where the words alone may be enough to steal funds [AZ]. The chapter also names the cost the scheme shares with BIP39: like it, Aezeed depends on both the backup and the recovery software supporting the same word list [AH].")), None),

 ("MuunCode", "Muun recovery code", 3, "CodeSchemes", ("a code that decrypts a printed kit",
   "The Muun recovery code is not words and is not the seed: it is a key that decrypts the private keys held in a document the wallet gives the user, which suits a wallet whose spending needs several signatures.",
   None), P(
  (W, "The Muun recovery code is the odd one in the chapter's list. The chapter describes it as a nonword code, used in the Muun wallet, which defaults to requiring that spending transactions be signed by multiple keys; the code must be accompanied by additional information, which Muun provides in a document, and the code is unrelated to the seed and is instead used to decrypt the private keys contained in that document [AH]. Everything a user needs is therefore in two parts that are useless apart."),
  (Y, "The arrangement follows from what the wallet is. A wallet whose spending requires several keys cannot be restored from one seed alone, because the other participants' public keys are not derivable from it, a point the chapter makes in its discussion of explicit paths [AH]. Packing the keys into a document and protecting them with a separate code solves that problem without asking the user to understand it. The chapter is honest about the price: this is unwieldy compared with the BIP39, Electrum version 2 and Aezeed codes, but it provides support for newer technologies and standards that are becoming more common in new wallets, such as the Lightning Network, output script descriptors and miniscript [AH]."),
  (E, "The scheme is met through Muun's own recovery tool, whose document says that the tool can be used to transfer all funds out of a Muun account to an address of the user's choosing, and that the process requires no collaboration from Muun to work [MU]. The instructions show how the two parts come together: the tool is run with the path to the user's kit document as its argument [MU]. The document also states that the tool is open source so that auditors can inspect the code, build their own binaries and verify them [MU]."),
  (H, "The mechanism is encryption rather than derivation. The private keys exist already; the document carries them in encrypted form; and the recovery code is the secret from which the decryption key is obtained. A user who holds both can decrypt the keys and sign a transaction that moves the funds elsewhere, which is why the recovery tool's single purpose is to sweep an account rather than to restore a working wallet. Because the keys are not derived from the code, changing the code means re-encrypting the document, not moving the money."),
  (K, "The scheme's weakness is the one its shape implies: two objects must both survive, and losing either loses the funds. Keeping the kit and the code in the same place also defeats the separation, exactly as the chapter warns for a passphrase kept with its recovery code [AH]. And because the code is specific to one wallet's design, it cannot be read by other software, so this is the clearest case of the lock-in that standards such as BIP39 and descriptors exist to prevent.")), None),

 ("Slip39Code", "SLIP39 share set", 3, "CodeSchemes", ("20 words for a 128-bit master secret",
   "SLIP39 splits one seed into several word codes with a threshold, so that any sufficient subset recovers the seed and any smaller subset reveals nothing about it.",
   ("200 // 10", "20")), P(
  (W, "SLIP39 is a successor to BIP39 with some of the same authors, and the chapter describes what it adds: a single seed can be distributed using multiple recovery codes that can be stored in different places or by different people, and when the codes are created the user specifies how many will be required to recover the seed, so that five codes may be made of which only three are needed [AH]. The standard itself describes an interoperable implementation of Shamir's secret sharing and a specification for its use in backing up hierarchical deterministic wallets [S39]."),
  (Y, "The scheme answers a tension that no single code can resolve. The standard puts it as the usual approach of redundant backups set against the risk that a backup holder absconds with an asset of significant and liquidable value, and says that secret sharing distributes custodianship among a number of trusted parties in a manner that can prevent loss even if one or a few of those parties are compromised [S39]. The chapter's own closing advice points the same way: either keep recovery codes very safe or use a method such as SLIP39 that lets a code be distributed across several locations without relying on the safety of any one of them [AH]."),
  (E, "The scheme is met in hardware signing devices from the company whose employees wrote it, and in the chapter's list of schemes in wide use [AH]. A reader also meets its structure in the standard's own example, in which a user splits an encrypted master secret two-of-four at the group level, keeps two group shares, splits one further three-of-five among friends and another two-of-six among family, so that even all six family members cannot recover the secret without three friends or one of the user's own shares [S39]. The worked example is the smallest standard size: the standard's table gives a 128-bit master secret a share value of 200 bits, which at ten bits a word, with a list of exactly 1,024 words, is the 20 words of one share [S39]."),
  (H, "The mathematics is polynomial interpolation over a finite field. The standard explains that a polynomial of degree less than the threshold is constructed and each party is given a point, an input and the matching output, so that any threshold-many points define the polynomial exactly; it stores the shared secret as the value at 255 and a digest of it at 254, so that a correct recovery can be verified [S39]. The sharing is applied to each byte of the secret separately in the field of 256 elements [S39]. Before splitting, the master secret is encrypted with the user's passphrase by a four-round Feistel network whose round function is PBKDF2, a construction the standard notes is invertible, so that an existing BIP39 wallet can be migrated into the scheme [S39]."),
  (K, "A threshold scheme moves risk rather than removing it. Too low a threshold makes theft easier, too high makes loss easier, and the chapter notes that SLIP39 depends on a global word list and does not directly provide versioning, the two complaints it also makes of BIP39 [AH]. The standard adds that there is no way to verify that the correct passphrase was used, which is deliberate, since it lets one encrypted secret yield several master secrets under different passphrases [S39]; the consequence for a user is the same lack of error detection the chapter describes for BIP39.")), None),

 ("Codex32Code", "codex32 recovery code", 3, "CodeSchemes", ("48 characters in the standard's own example",
   "codex32 writes a BIP32 master seed in the bech32 alphabet with an error-correcting checksum that can be verified by hand, and can split the seed into up to 31 shares with a threshold between 2 and 9.",
   ("len('ms10testsxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx4nzvca9cmczlw')", "48")), P(
  (W, "codex32 is the proposal the chapter adds in a note beside the five schemes. It describes a new system for distributing recovery codes with similarities to SLIP39, which allows creating and validating codes with nothing except printed instructions, scissors, a precision knife, brass fasteners and a pen, plus privacy and a few hours of spare time, while those who trust computers can do it instantly in software; up to 31 codes can be created with a stated number required to recover the seed [AH]. The proposal itself, BIP93, calls the format a checksummed base32 format and a standard for backing up and restoring the master seed of a BIP32 hierarchical deterministic wallet [B93]."),
  (Y, "The proposal exists for the kind of user who does not want to trust a device at all. Its motivation says that safely storing the master seed is the hardest and most important part of self-custody, and that there is a tension between security, which demands limiting the number of backups, and resilience, which demands widely replicated backups, which encrypting the seed does not resolve because the encryption key raises the same problem [B93]. Because all computations can be done with simple lookup tables, it states in so many words that checksums can be computed and verified, and seeds split and recovered, entirely using pen and paper [B93]."),
  (E, "A reader meets codex32 first as a string. The worked example counts the characters of the standard's own first test vector, ms10testsxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx4nzvca9cmczlw, and finds 48: three for the prefix, one threshold character, four identifier characters, one share index, 26 data characters and 13 of checksum [B93]. The chapter asks readers interested in distributed recovery codes to investigate the proposal's current status, because details might change significantly before the book was published [AH]; the BIPs repository still gives BIP93 the status Draft [B93]."),
  (H, "The checksum is what makes the hand computation possible. The standard uses the bech32 alphabet rather than a word list, giving fixed-length compact encodings, and a BCH error-correcting checksum rather than a hash; the ability to compute properties by hand comes, it says, from the choice of a small field and the use of linear error-correcting codes, and comes with no reduction in security as long as users use high-quality randomness [B93]. The price of that choice is stated as plainly: because hand computation must be supported, the standard does not support passphrases or key hardening, and it does not support splitting shares further [B93]."),
  (K, "The proposal is explicitly not a finished standard, and a course should treat it as one more point on the trade-off curve rather than as a recommendation. Its own comparison with BIP39 is the sharpest summary of why such proposals exist: BIP39 has no error-correcting ability, cannot sensibly be extended to support secret sharing, has no support for versioning or other metadata, and has technical design decisions that make interoperability difficult, among them the use of SHA-512 to derive seeds and the use of 11-bit words [B93]. Every one of those sentences is a design decision a student can now recognise in the earlier sections.")), None),

 ("CodeTradeoffs", "Choices behind a recovery code", 2, "RecoveryCodes", None, P(
  (W, "This group gathers the decisions that are not settled by choosing a scheme. Whether to add a passphrase, whether plausible deniability is a feature or a trap, why a phrase a person invents is not a recovery code, how far memory can be trusted, what a code is worth to someone prepared to use force, and whether the code records when the wallet was created: each of these is discussed in the chapter, and each applies across several of the schemes [AH]."),
  (Y, "The decisions matter because they are the ones a user actually makes, and because they cannot all be made well at once. The chapter's sidebar on recovery code passphrases says that many users and developers disagree about which approach is better, with some strongly in favour of plausible deniability and others preferring the increased safety that error detection gives novice users and those under duress, and it expects the debate to continue as long as recovery codes are widely used [AH]. A course that presented one answer would misrepresent the field."),
  (H, "The sections of the group move from the mechanism to the person. The passphrase section explains the second factor that three of the schemes add without covering it by a checksum; deniability and memory are the two properties users most often misjudge; the brainwallet section explains why randomness must come from the wallet and not the user; coercion is the risk that the chapter documents with a published list of attacks; and the wallet birthday is the small piece of metadata that makes a restoration cheap. Read together, they explain why the chapter's recommendation is to write a code down and to test the backup.")), None),

 ("RecoveryPassphrase", "Recovery code passphrase", 3, "CodeTradeoffs", ("a different seed for every passphrase",
   "A passphrase is an extra secret that is mixed into the derivation of the seed, so that one recovery code yields a different wallet for every passphrase and the code alone is not enough to spend.",
   (PROG(PBKDF2 + "result = seed(b'%s', b'mnemonic' + b'typo').hex()[:16]" % MN12), "'7cb305fd619db8de'")), P(
  (W, "A passphrase, in the context of a recovery code, is an additional secret that the user supplies and that enters the computation of the seed. The chapter says that the BIP39, Electrum version 2, Aezeed and SLIP39 schemes may all be used with an optional passphrase, and that if the only place the passphrase is kept is the user's memory it has the same advantages and disadvantages as memorising the code itself [AH]. In BIP39 the passphrase is appended to a fixed string to form the salt of the key-stretching function, which the seed derivation group explains in detail [B39]."),
  (Y, "The passphrase exists to make a stolen code useless. The chapter names the two features it creates: a second factor, something memorised, which protects a recovery code from compromise by a casual thief, though protection from a tech-savvy thief needs a very strong passphrase; and a form of plausible deniability or duress wallet, where a chosen passphrase leads to a wallet with a small amount of funds that distracts an attacker from the real one [AH]. The first of these is the reason a passphrase is worth considering at all; the second is the subject of its own section."),
  (E, "A user meets the passphrase at two moments: when a wallet offers one at creation, and when a restoration asks for it. The chapter's own tables show what it changes: the same 12-word code gives the seed beginning 5b56c417 with no passphrase and the seed beginning 3b5df16d with the passphrase SuperDuperSecret [AH]. The worked example extends the same computation to a passphrase of four letters, typo, and gets a third, unrelated seed, which is the point of the next paragraph."),
  (H, "The mechanism is that the passphrase is an input and not a check. In BIP39 the key-stretching function takes the words as its password and the string 'mnemonic' followed by the passphrase as its salt, so any passphrase at all produces some 512-bit seed [B39]. The chapter draws the conclusion for the user: given a single recovery code, every possible passphrase leads to a different seed, so there is essentially no wrong passphrase, all passphrases are valid and they all lead to different seeds, forming a vast set of possible uninitialised wallets [AH]. Three of the four schemes do not include the passphrase in the checksum they use against data-entry mistakes, so a wrong passphrase cannot be detected; Aezeed, which authenticates its passphrase, is the exception [AH]."),
  (K, "The passphrase introduces a new way to lose everything, which the chapter states twice. If the wallet owner is incapacitated or dead and nobody else knows the passphrase, the seed is useless and the funds are lost forever; and if the owner backs the passphrase up in the same place as the code, the second factor is defeated [AH]. The chapter's conclusion is that passphrases should be used only together with a carefully planned process for backup and recovery, one that considers the possibility of outliving the owner and allowing the family to recover the estate [AH]. A further cost is the reduced error detection: a slightly wrong passphrase shows a zero balance, which a novice may read as permanent loss, or may not notice for years [AH].")), None),

 ("PlausibleDeniability", "Plausible deniability", 3, "CodeTradeoffs", ("a valid tree under every passphrase",
   "Plausible deniability is the property that a revealed recovery code or passphrase always looks valid, so a holder cannot be shown to have withheld anything - which protects and endangers them at the same time.",
   None), P(
  (W, "Plausible deniability is the chapter's name for the ability to create multiple passphrases for the same recovery code that all look valid [AH]. Because every passphrase yields a working tree of keys, a user who hands over a code, with or without a passphrase, cannot be proved to have handed over the one that holds the money. BIP39's own text claims the property in the same words, saying that the method provides plausible deniability because every passphrase generates a valid seed, and thus a deterministic wallet, but only the correct one makes the desired wallet available [B39]."),
  (Y, "The property matters because recovery codes are sometimes taken by force rather than stolen quietly. The chapter sets out the positive case: if someone obtains a code but not the passphrase, they see a valid tree of keys, and a user who prepared for that contingency by sending some bitcoins to the passphrase-free tree will have that money stolen, which is a bad thing in itself but also a warning that the code has been compromised, allowing the user to investigate and take corrective measures [AH]."),
  (E, "A learner meets the idea under two names. As a feature of a scheme, it follows from a passphrase that is not authenticated, which is the case in BIP39, Electrum version 2 and SLIP39; SLIP39's standard says explicitly that there is no way to verify that the correct passphrase was used, which allows the user to obtain multiple master secrets from a single encrypted secret [S39]. As a wallet design, it is the duress wallet the chapter names among the features a passphrase creates: a chosen passphrase that leads to a wallet holding a small amount intended to distract an attacker from the real one [AH]."),
  (H, "The mechanism is the absence of a check, so the property cannot be had together with error detection. Aezeed shows the trade-off from the other side: because it authenticates the passphrase and returns an error for a wrong one, the chapter says it eliminates plausible deniability, adds error detection and makes it possible to prove that the passphrase has been revealed [AH]. Which of those three is an advantage depends entirely on who is asking for the passphrase, and that is why the chapter presents the question as an open disagreement among users and developers rather than as a solved problem [AH]."),
  (K, "The danger of the property is as concrete as its benefit, and the chapter states it without softening: if a user is coerced into giving an attacker a code and it does not yield the amount of bitcoins the attacker expected, the attacker may go on coercing them until a different passphrase with access to more money is given, and designing for deniability means there is no way to prove that everything has been revealed, so coercion may continue even after it has [AH]. A learner should take from this that deniability is a defence against a thief who has left, not against one who is still present.")), None),

 ("Brainwallet", "Brainwallet", 3, "CodeTradeoffs", ("132 bits in twelve random words",
   "A brainwallet is a wallet whose keys come from a phrase the user chose, which is insecure because people are poor sources of randomness; a recovery code is generated by the wallet instead.",
   ("(2048 ** 12).bit_length() - 1", "132")), P(
  (W, "A brainwallet is a wallet whose seed comes from a phrase that the user thought up. The chapter raises the term only to separate it from a recovery code: recovery codes are often confused with brainwallets, it says, and they are not the same, the primary difference being that a brainwallet consists of words chosen by the user whereas recovery codes are created randomly by the wallet and presented to the user [AH]. BIP39 makes the same separation in its motivation, saying that the guide is a way to transport computer-generated randomness with a human-readable transcription and not a way to process user-created sentences, also known as brainwallets, into a wallet seed [B39]."),
  (Y, "The distinction decides whether the keys can be guessed. The chapter gives the reason in one clause: the difference makes recovery codes much more secure because humans are very poor sources of randomness [AH]. A phrase a person invents is drawn from a small space of memorable phrases that an attacker can enumerate, and an attacker who can enumerate it needs no access to the user at all, because the public blockchain shows which addresses hold money. The entropy group of this chapter returns to this as a general rule about where randomness must come from."),
  (E, "The term is met mostly as a historical warning, in older guides that invited users to remember a sentence, and in the chapter's tip that separates the two ideas [AH]. It is also met implicitly whenever a wallet refuses to let a user type their own words: that refusal is the scheme protecting the user from themselves. The owner's course notes for this chapter devote a slide pair to the same comparison, under the heading brainwallets against mnemonics [AL]."),
  (H, "The worked example measures what the wallet's randomness buys. A 12-word BIP39 code is chosen from a list of 2,048 words, so a code drawn uniformly stands for one of 2,048 to the twelfth possibilities, and that number has 132 bits, of which 128 are entropy and 4 the checksum [B39]. A phrase that a person composes has no such guarantee: its space is whatever an attacker's dictionary and grammar can generate, which is many orders of magnitude smaller, and no key-stretching function closes a gap of that size, as the chapter notes when it says that key stretching helps only when an attacker already has to search a large space [AH]."),
  (K, "The term is sometimes used loosely for memorising a recovery code, and the two should not be mixed up: memorising a code the wallet generated keeps the wallet's randomness and risks only forgetting, whereas inventing a phrase destroys the randomness. The chapter's recommendation covers both cases from the other side, strongly encouraging a reader to write the code down even when the scheme was designed for easy memorisation [AH].")), None),

 ("Memorization", "Memorising a recovery code", 3, "CodeTradeoffs", ("words instead of hexadecimal",
   "Memorising a code is possible because the code is words, and is dangerous because memory fails, cannot be inherited and can be coerced; the chapter recommends writing the code down as well.",
   None), P(
  (W, "Memorising a recovery code means keeping it only in one's head. The chapter says that a large part of the motivation for using words was to make a recovery code easy to remember, and it prints the same code in hexadecimal and in English words so that a reader can judge which they would rather memorise [AH]. The ability is therefore a side effect of the encoding, not a recommended practice."),
  (Y, "There is one situation in which it is genuinely valuable, and the chapter names it: there may be cases where remembering a code is a powerful feature, such as when a person cannot transport physical belongings, like a code written on paper, without their being seized or inspected by an outside party that might steal the bitcoins [AH]. For a reader in a stable situation this is an edge case; for a reader crossing a border under pressure it may be the only option."),
  (E, "The question is met whenever a wallet shows its words for the first time and asks the user to confirm them. It is also met in the schemes themselves: Electrum calls its optional extra secret a seed extension and the chapter notes that keeping a passphrase only in memory has the same advantages and disadvantages as memorising the code [AH] [EL]. The worked example of this section is the comparison the chapter prints: 32 hexadecimal groups against 12 ordinary English words for the same 128 bits."),
  (H, "The mechanism is simply that 12 to 24 dictionary words are within the reach of ordinary rote learning, where 32 hexadecimal digits are not, and that the word list was designed to help: BIP39 says an ideal word list is chosen so that the first four letters identify a word unambiguously, avoids similar pairs such as build and built or woman and women, which make a sentence harder to remember and more error-prone, and is sorted so that implementations can search it efficiently [B39]. None of this makes the memory itself reliable."),
  (K, "The chapter lists three failure modes, and they are the reason it advises against relying on memory alone. If the code is forgotten and the original wallet database is lost, the bitcoins are lost forever; if the owner dies or is severely injured and the heirs have no access to the wallet database, they cannot inherit; and if someone believes that a code is memorised, they may try to coerce the user into disclosing it [AH]. Its own conclusion is explicit: even with a scheme designed for easy memorisation, the authors very strongly encourage a reader to consider writing the code down [AH].")), None),

 ("PhysicalCoercion", "Coercion and physical risk", 3, "CodeTradeoffs", ("a published record of attacks",
   "Because a recovery code is all that is needed to spend, people who are believed to hold one have been attacked in person; the chapter cites a public list of such cases and the list has grown since.",
   None), P(
  (W, "Coercion is the risk that someone obtains a recovery code by threatening its holder rather than by breaking anything. The chapter raises it in the list of reasons not to rely on memory: if someone thinks a user has a code memorised that will give them access to bitcoins, they may attempt to coerce that user into disclosing it, and the chapter supports the point by citing a record kept by the Bitcoin contributor Jameson Lopp, documenting over 100 physical attacks against suspected owners of bitcoin and other digital assets, including at least three deaths and numerous occasions where someone was tortured, held hostage, or had their family threatened [AH] [LO]."),
  (Y, "The risk belongs in a technical chapter because it follows from a technical fact. Bitcoin payments are irreversible and a key cannot be frozen or reissued, so a code is a bearer instrument: whoever can state it can spend. Every design decision in this branch therefore has a human consequence as well as a cryptographic one, and the chapter treats the two together, which is why it discusses deniability as a safety question and not only as a property of a hash function."),
  (E, "A learner meets the subject in the chapter's cautions and in the list the chapter points to, whose saved copy for this course describes itself as a list of known attacks against Bitcoin and crypto-asset owning entities that occurred in meatspace, and notes that it is not comprehensive because many attacks are not publicly reported [LO]. The copy read for this chapter is longer than the figure the book printed: its table holds 364 rows, which is checked by a claim of this chapter that counts them, so the chapter's over 100 is a floor and not a current count [LO]."),
  (H, "The defences available are organisational rather than mathematical, and the chapter names them in passing. A duress wallet with a small balance can satisfy a casual attacker, at the risk the deniability section describes; distributing a code with SLIP39 or codex32 means that no one place and no one person can be made to produce the whole secret; and not being believed to hold a code at all is the most effective defence of the three, which is why the chapter treats privacy and recovery as related subjects [AH] [S39] [B93]."),
  (K, "Two things should be said carefully. The first is that the published figures are a lower bound on reported cases and not a measurement of risk for any individual, since the list itself says it is incomplete [LO]. The second is that a scheme chosen for resistance to coercion can increase the danger, as the chapter's own analysis of deniability shows: when nothing can be proved to have been fully revealed, there is also nothing that ends the demand [AH].")), None),

 ("WalletBirthday", "Wallet birthday", 3, "CodeTradeoffs", ("a date in two bytes",
   "A wallet birthday is the creation date recorded inside a recovery code, so that a restoration can scan the blockchain from that date instead of from the beginning.",
   ("2009 + 2 ** 16 // 365", "2188")), P(
  (W, "A wallet birthday is the date on which the wallet database was created, stored inside the recovery code itself. The chapter names it as one of the two features Aezeed adds over BIP39: a reference to the date when the user created the wallet database, which allows a restoration process to find all of the funds associated with a wallet without scanning the entire blockchain, something it says is especially useful for privacy-focused lightweight clients [AH]. The idea is metadata rather than cryptography: nothing is protected by it, and a restoration is simply made cheaper."),
  (Y, "Without it, a restoration has to assume the worst. A wallet that does not know when its keys were first used must examine the whole chain, which for a lightweight client means either downloading a great deal of data or asking a server questions that reveal which addresses are of interest. The Aezeed document gives the same reason from the implementer's side: without a birthday, wallets do not know how far back to look in the chain to ensure that they derive all of the proper user addresses [AZ]."),
  (E, "A reader meets the birthday during an lnd recovery, where the log line the recovery document prints says that the seed birthday has been surpassed and that recovery of the wallet is starting from a particular height with a recovery window, and the document notes that the rescan's duration depends on how old the wallet is, because the cipher seed stores the wallet's birthday [LR]. The same document says that without a birthday a wallet would have to be rescanned back to the genesis block [LR]."),
  (H, "The field is small because the resolution needed is coarse. Aezeed's plaintext is one byte of internal version, two bytes of timestamp and sixteen bytes of entropy, and the document explains that the two-byte timestamp is expressed in Bitcoin days genesis, the number of days since the timestamp in Bitcoin's genesis block, which saves space and avoids a wasteful level of granularity; it adds that this can currently express time up until 2188 [AZ]. The worked example checks that claim: two bytes hold 65,536 values, which at 365 days a year is 179 years, and 2009 plus 179 is 2188."),
  (K, "A birthday is a privacy hazard as well as a convenience: it narrows the window in which a wallet's transactions can be looked for, and a code that carries it tells anyone who reads the code roughly when its owner started. It is also absent from the schemes in widest use, so a BIP39 restoration still has to scan broadly; the gap limit section of this chapter describes the other half of that problem, which is not when to start scanning but how far to go.")), None),
]

